      "fragment_index": 3,
      "start": 139.16,
      "end": 209.96,
      "duration": 70.8,
      "text": "The same principles must guide the approach to AI and information accessibility. The challenge before us is not a technological challenge, it's fundamentally a political and economic one. AI systems capable of revolutionizing information access already exists. What's missing, however, is the policy architecture to ensure these capabilities reach those who need them most. In my work, I witnessed how policy can either accelerate or obstruct technological inclusion. When Estonia pioneered digital governance making government services accessible online to all citizens, they didn't just adopt technology. They redesigned public service delivery around digital inclusion principles. In Finland, guaranteed broadband internet as a legal right, they recognized connectivity as essential infrastructure, not an add-on commercial luxury. These examples illuminate our path forward.",
