      "text": "AI for information accessibility requires similar bold policy commitments, treating AI literacy as a public good, mandating accessibility features in AI systems, accessing public information and creating public infrastructure that serve citizens rather than shareholders. Working with private sector stakeholders across Europe, I've learned that the most successful AI accessibility initiatives emerge not from regulatory pressure alone, but recognizing the enormous market opportunity in inclusive design. When Microsoft invested heavily in AI powered accessibility features, they didn't just serve users with disability, they created technologies that benefit everyone. Parents then multitask with audio content professionals that work in noise environments, international audiences accessing content across language barriers. This is the power of universal design in AI. Solutions created for accessibility often deliver breakthrough innovations for broader",
